  <main><section class="article-hero section-pad"><a class="back-link" href="/#stories">← All research stories</a><p class="eyebrow">RESEARCH STORY · ANTARCTICA</p><h1>Learning to see<br />change <em>in slow motion.</em></h1><p class="article-deck">Old photographs can hold a surprisingly long view of a glacier. The challenge is learning how to compare them carefully.</p><p class="article-byline">A plain-language introduction to research co-authored by Sarah F. Child.</p></section>
    <article class="article-body"><p class="article-lead">A glacier can look still in a single photograph. Compare images separated by years or decades, though, and the ice begins to tell a different story.</p><p>For Kennicott and Root Glaciers in Alaska, researchers combined historical photographs, elevation records, and new bed topography measurements to build an 85-year history. Sarah Child co-authored the study, which follows change from 1938 through 2023.</p><h2>Why look back?</h2><p>At the glacier terminus, the team observed little change in the two decades before 1957, followed by ongoing and accelerating mass loss. A record stretching beyond recent satellite observations helps show how different parts of a glacier have changed over time.</p><p>The longer history also matters for projections. For 2100, the authors report projected mass losses of 38–63% for Kennicott Glacier and 38–58% for Root Glacier relative to 2000, depending on the emissions scenario. Estimates based on their historical constraints differed by up to 22% from projections calibrated only with the most recent two decades of change.</p><aside class="article-callout">What happened before the satellite era can change how scientists estimate what may happen next.</aside><h2>From image to evidence</h2><p>Using old photographs is not as simple as flipping between two pictures. Researchers need to account for camera angle, terrain, scale, and the way each image was captured. When aligned with other elevation and bed data, historical imagery can become part of a measured glacier record.</p><p>The projections are scenario-dependent, not a single prediction. The study shows why longer observational histories can improve estimates of glacier change and future mass loss.</p><a class="button button-dark" href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12373804/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full paper <span>↗</span></a><p class="citation">Wells, A. et al. (2025). “An 85-year record of glacier change and refined projections for Kennicott and Root Glaciers, Alaska.” <em>Nature Communications</em>.</p></article>
